-- title:
--   Equivariant embedding of ̄ F into the completed function field
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ denotes `AlgebraicClosure ℚ`, $C := \widehat{A_2}$ denotes the completion `A₂.valuation.Completion` of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ at the valuation of a valuation subring $A_2$, and $\mathcal O_C :=$ [`ValuationSubring.ratClosure A₂`](def/ValuationSubring_CompletionRatClosure.html#L13) is the closure of the prime subfield inside $C$. Writing $\theta : \mathcal O_0 \to C$ for the composite of the structure map $\mathcal O_0 \to \mathcal O_C$ with $\mathcal O_C \hookrightarrow C$, put $\mathcal X_C := \mathcal X_0 \times_{\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O_0} \operatorname{Spec} C$ (the categorical pullback of $f_0$ along $\operatorname{Spec}\theta$) and, for each prime $\ell$ distinct from $q,q'$, $\mathcal Y_{\ell,C} := \mathcal Y_0(\ell) \times_{\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O_0} \operatorname{Spec} C$; both are assumed integral (the instance hypotheses `hXCint`, `hYCint`), so their function fields are defined, and $\iota_C : C \to K(\mathcal X_C)$ denotes [`AlgebraicCurve.baseToFunctionField`](def/AlgebraicCurve_CurveModel.html#L18) of the second projection, namely the germ at the generic point of the structure map.
--
--   *Numerical data.* Natural numbers $N \neq 0$ squarefree, primes $q, q'$ with $q \nmid N$, $q' \nmid N$, $q' \neq q$, $q \ge 5$, $q' \ge 5$, and a non-zero $D$ with $2Nqq' \mid D$. The valuation subring $A_2 \subset \overline{\mathbb Q}$ satisfies `LiesOverPrime q`, i.e. $q$ is a non-unit of $A_2$.
--
--   *Definite quaternionic data.* Rationals $a_1,b_1$ with `IsDefiniteRamifiedExactlyAt q'`: $a_1<0$, $b_1<0$, and for each finite place $v$ of $\mathbb Q$ the algebra $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]\otimes_{\mathbb Q} \mathbb Q_v$ is a division algebra exactly when $q' \in v$. Further: $\mathbb Z$-lattices $\Lambda_1, R_1$ in $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]$ with $\Lambda_1$ a maximal order, $R_1$ an Eichler order of level $N$ (an intersection of two maximal orders of relative index $N$ in the first), and $R_1 \le \Lambda_1$; a finite-idelic unit $n_1$ lying in `primeHeckeSet R₁ q` (the four-clause condition on the adelic box of $R_1$); the hypotheses that $R_1 \cap n_1 R_1 n_1^{-1}$ (`meetOrder R₁ n₁`) is an Eichler order of level $Nq$, that conjugation by $n_1$ preserves it, and that the shift by $n_1$ on the class set of its finite-idelic stabiliser is an involution; finiteness and decidability instances for the two class sets; and `hlaws₁ : ClassSetHeckeLaws N q Λ₁ R₁ n₁`, the conjunction of four clauses (pairwise commutation of the edge Hecke matrices, pairwise commutation of the vertex Hecke matrices, intertwining of edge and vertex Hecke operators by the two degeneracy push-forwards at primes $\ell \neq q$, and stability of their joint kernel under the edge Hecke operators).
--
--   *Indefinite quaternionic data and the complex-analytic side.* Rationals $a,b$ with `IsIndefiniteRamifiedExactlyAt a b q q'` ($0<a$ or $0<b$, and $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a,b]\otimes\mathbb Q_v$ is a division algebra exactly at the places above $q$ and $q'$), and a maximal order $\Lambda$ in it. A field $\bar F =$ `Fbar` over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, which is a curve over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ (principal divisors of degree zero, residue fields of finite degree, $\Omega_{\bar F/\overline{\mathbb Q}}$ free of rank one) and essentially of finite type. A scheme $X$ with a morphism $\pi_X$ to $\operatorname{Spec}\mathbb Z[1/D]$, a $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-point $\bar s$ of that base, and a point assignment `pt` sending each fake elliptic curve with $\Lambda$-action and level-$N$ structure over a commutative ring $S$ to a section of $\pi_X$ over a given $s$, subject to the four clauses `pt_iso`, `pt_pullback`, `pt_surjective`, `pt_injective` (invariance under isomorphism, compatibility with base change along ring maps, and bijectivity on geometric points of algebraically closed fields) — that is, all of `IsCoarseModuli` except the universal property. A curve model $\mathfrak M$ of $\bar F$ over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, an isomorphism $e_{\mathfrak M}$ from $\mathfrak M.C$ to the pullback of $\pi_X$ along $\bar s$ with $e_{\mathfrak M}$ followed by the second projection equal to $\mathfrak M.\mathrm{toBase}$, a homomorphism `gal` from $\operatorname{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q)$ to the semilinear automorphisms of $\bar F$ over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, tower data $\mathbb T$ (fields $\mathbb T.F(\ell)$, curves over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, with finite integral maps $\mathbb T.\varphi(\ell,k) : \bar F \to \mathbb T.F(\ell)$ for $k \in \{0,1\}$), Galois actions `galT` on the tower fields, and semilinear automorphisms $W_0,W_1$ of $\bar F$ and $WT$ of the tower fields. Finally `tw`, a witness of type `ModuliTowerWitnessD Λ N q q' D Fbar X πX sbar pt 𝔐 e𝔐 gal 𝕋 galT W WT`: it provides fake elliptic curves representing the places of $\bar F$ compatibly with `pt` and $\mathfrak M$, representatives with extra level $\ell$ for the places of $\mathbb T.F(\ell)$, bijective up to isomorphism, the statements that `gal`, `galT`, $W$, $WT$ induce on $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ the given Galois element resp. the identity, and the matching of the two degeneracy maps with level restriction and level isogeny, together with further compatibility clauses, summarised here.
--
--   *Local data at $q$.* The instance `hiso₂` that every element of the decomposition subgroup of $A_2$ over $\mathbb Q$ preserves the valuation; a height-one prime $v_2$ of $\mathbb Z$ containing $q$; an injective $\mathbb Q$-algebra map $\iota_2 : \mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1] \to M_2(\mathcal O_C)$ and the homomorphism $\rho_2$ to $PGL_2(\mathcal O_C)$ induced by it; a pseudo-uniformiser $\varpi_2$ for $\mathcal O_C \subset C$ whose image in $C$ is $q$, and the hypothesis that the associated ring of holomorphic functions `HolRingOf ϖ₂ ρ₂` is a domain; elements $s_2(\ell) \in \mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]^\times$ and finite ideles $sf_2(\ell)$ for each prime $\ell \neq q,q'$, with `hs₂` asserting that $sf_2(\ell)$ has component $s_2(\ell)\otimes 1$ at every place not above $q$, component $1$ at places above $q$, that the diagonal idele of $\ell$ times $sf_2(\ell)^{-1}$ lies in `levelHeckeUSet Λ₁ (meetOrder R₁ n₁) ℓ` if $\ell \mid N$ and in `primeHeckeSet (meetOrder R₁ n₁) ℓ` otherwise, and that $\mathrm{nrd}(s_2(\ell)) = \ell$; subgroups $\Gamma_2$ indexed by `HeckeTower.Obj q q'`, with $\Gamma_2(\mathrm{none})$ the group of units lying in `awayUnits R₁ v₂` (locally in the group generated by the unit group of the local box of $R_1$ at every finite place $\neq v_2$) whose reduced norm has even $q$-adic valuation, and $\Gamma_2(\ell) = \Gamma_2(\mathrm{none}) \cap s_2(\ell)\Gamma_2(\mathrm{none})s_2(\ell)^{-1}$; elements $w_2, \bar w_2$ indexed the same way, with `hw₂` requiring $w_2(\mathrm{none}) \in$ `awayUnits R₁ v₂` of reduced norm $q$ and $w_2(\ell) \in$ `awayUnits (meetOrder R₁ (sf₂ ℓ)) v₂` of reduced norm $q$, and `hwbar₂` requiring each $\bar w_2$ to have reduced norm $q'$, to be locally a box unit at the places $u \neq v_2$ not above $q'$, and to normalise by conjugation the local boxes of $R_1$ (resp. of `meetOrder R₁ (sf₂ ℓ)`) and of $\Lambda_1$ at all $u \neq v_2$; a homomorphism $d_{\mathrm{Iso}2}$ from the decomposition subgroup to the isometric automorphisms of $C$ over $\mathcal O_C$, realising the Galois action; and a character $\chi$ of the decomposition subgroup with values in $\mathbb Z/2$ which is trivial on inertia, non-trivial on every Frobenius at $q$, and whose kernel consists of the elements fixing all $x$ in the residue field of $A_2$ with $x^{q^2} = x$.
--
--   *Coefficient rings.* A characteristic-zero domain $\mathcal O_0$ which is a discrete valuation ring with irreducible $\pi$, $\pi$-adically complete, of residue cardinality $q$ and with $(q) = (\pi)$, equipped with an algebra structure making $\mathcal O_C$ its fraction field; a characteristic-zero domain $\mathrm{Onr}$ over $\mathcal O_0$ with an $\mathcal O_0$-algebra automorphism $\mathrm{Fr}$, $\pi$-adically complete, with $(\pi)$ maximal, every element a root of a monic $\mathcal O_0$-polynomial modulo $\pi$, every monic polynomial of positive degree having a root modulo $\pi$, and $\mathrm{Fr}(x) \equiv x^q \pmod \pi$; a homomorphism $v_{\det}$ from $GL_2(\mathcal O_C)$ to $\mathbb Z$ characterised by $v_{\det}(g) = n$ iff $\det g$ is a unit of $\mathcal O_0$ times $\pi^n$; a ring map $\psi_0 : \mathrm{Onr} \to C$ with values of valuation $\le 1$ extending $\mathcal O_0 \to C$; and the hypotheses that the image of $\pi$ in $\mathcal O_C$ is $\varpi_2.\varpi$, that $N$ is invertible in $\mathcal O_0$, that the image of $\mathcal O_0$ in $\mathcal O_C$ is exactly the set of elements of valuation $\le 1$, and the rank-one condition that for $v(x) < 1$ and $y \neq 0$ some power $v(x)^n$ is $\le v(y)$.
--
--   *Integral models.* A scheme $\mathcal X_0$ with $f_0 : \mathcal X_0 \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O_0$ and a point assignment $\mathrm{pt}_0$ making $(\mathcal X_0, f_0, \mathrm{pt}_0)$ a coarse moduli space (all five clauses of `IsCoarseModuli Λ N`, including the universal property), with $\mathcal X_0$ integral and $f_0$ flat, separated, locally of finite type and quasi-compact; for each $\ell$ a coarse moduli space $(\mathcal Y_0(\ell), g_0(\ell), \mathrm{ptT}_0(\ell))$ for objects with extra level-$\ell$ structure; degeneracy maps $d_{0}(\ell), d_{1}(\ell) : \mathcal Y_0(\ell) \to \mathcal X_0$ over $\mathcal O_0$, the first inducing on points the forgetting of the extra level and the second the passage to an $\ell$-level isogeny quotient; endomorphisms $\mathrm{ar}, \overline{\mathrm{ar}}$ of $\mathcal X_0$ over $\mathcal O_0$ inducing on points the Atkin–Lehner quotients at $q$ and at $q'$.
--
--   *Čerednik–Drinfeld uniformisation data.* Maps $\Theta$ and $\Theta_T(\ell)$ which, for every $\mathcal O_0$-algebra $B$ in which $\pi$ is nilpotent, send a pair consisting of an $\mathcal O_0$-algebra map $\mathrm{Onr} \to B$ and a Deligne datum over $B$ (the functor `FormalOmega.Omega` relative to $\mathcal O_C$ and $\pi$) to a $\pi$-nilpotent point of $f_0$, resp. of $g_0(\ell)$. The hypothesis `hCD` is the conjunction of nine clauses: naturality of $\Theta$ and of each $\Theta_T(\ell)$ in $B$; invariance of $\Theta$ under the twisted action (`IsTwistedAct`, the Frobenius twist by $-v_{\det}$ on the $\mathrm{Onr}$-coordinate together with pullback of the Deligne datum) of the elements of `awayUnits R₁ v₂`, and of $\Theta_T(\ell)$ under that subgroup intersected with its $s_2(\ell)$-conjugate; for every algebraically closed $\mathcal O_0$-algebra field $k$ with $\pi$ nilpotent and every $\psi : \mathrm{Onr} \to k$, surjectivity of $\Theta(\psi,-)$ onto the $\pi$-nilpotent points, with fibres exactly the orbits of that twisted action, and likewise for $\Theta_T(\ell)$; the universal property that any natural, invariant family $\rho$ with values in the $\pi$-nilpotent points of some $t$ factors uniquely through $\Theta$, resp. through $\Theta_T(\ell)$; the compatibility of $\Theta_T(\ell)$ with $d_0(\ell)$ (it recovers $\Theta$) and with $d_1(\ell)$ (it recovers $\Theta$ of any point carried to the given one by the twisted action of $s_2(\ell)$); and the compatibility of $\mathrm{ar}$ with the Frobenius twist by $1$ on the $\mathrm{Onr}$-coordinate, and of $\overline{\mathrm{ar}}$ with the twisted action of $\bar w_2(\mathrm{none})$.
--
--   *Conclusion.* There exists a ring homomorphism $j : \bar F \to K(\mathcal X_C)$ with the following seven properties.
--
--   (1) For every $z \in \overline{\mathbb Q}$, $j$ of the image of $z$ in $\bar F$ equals $\iota_C$ of the image of $z$ in $C$.
--
--   (2) The subfield of $K(\mathcal X_C)$ generated by the union of the range of $\iota_C$ and the range of $j$ is all of $K(\mathcal X_C)$.
--
--   (3) Linear disjointness over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$: for every finite subset $t \subset \bar F$ whose elements are linearly independent over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ and every family of coefficients $c : t \to C$, the relation $\sum_{x \in t} \iota_C(c_x)\, j(x) = 0$ forces $c_x = 0$ for all $x \in t$.
--
--   (4) Equivariance for $W_0$ and the Atkin–Lehner map at $q$: for every endomorphism $a$ of $\mathcal X_C$ with $a$ followed by the first projection equal to the first projection followed by $\mathrm{ar}$, and $a$ followed by the second projection equal to the second projection, and for every $x \in \bar F$: whenever $U$ is an open of $\mathcal X_C$ containing the generic point, the generic point also lies in $a^{-1}U$, and a section $\sigma$ over $U$ has germ $j(x)$ at the generic point, then the germ at the generic point of the image of $\sigma$ under $a$ on $a^{-1}U$ is $j(W_0 \cdot x)$.
--
--   (5) The same statement with $\overline{\mathrm{ar}}$ in place of $\mathrm{ar}$ and $W_1 \cdot x$ in place of $W_0 \cdot x$.
--
--   (6) Galois equivariance: for every $\tau$ in the decomposition subgroup of $A_2$ over $\mathbb Q$ and every endomorphism $t_C$ of $\mathcal X_C$ with $t_C$ followed by the first projection equal to the first projection, and $t_C$ followed by the second projection equal to the second projection followed by $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the ring automorphism $d_{\mathrm{Iso}2}(\tau)$ of $C$, the germ formulation of (4) holds with the conclusion $j\big(\mathrm{gal}(\tau)\cdot x\big)$, where $\tau$ is regarded as an element of $\operatorname{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q)$.
--
--   (7) Compatibility with the tower: for every prime $\ell$ distinct from $q$ and $q'$ there is a ring homomorphism $j_\ell : \mathbb T.F(\ell) \to K(\mathcal Y_{\ell,C})$ such that for each $k \in \{0,1\}$ and each morphism $d_C : \mathcal Y_{\ell,C} \to \mathcal X_C$ with $d_C$ followed by the first projection equal to the first projection of $\mathcal Y_{\ell,C}$ followed by $d_0(\ell)$ if $k = 0$ and by $d_1(\ell)$ if $k = 1$, and $d_C$ followed by the second projection equal to the second projection, the germ formulation of (4) holds for $d_C$ with the conclusion $j_\ell\big(\mathbb T.\varphi(\ell,k)(x)\big)$.
--
--   This is the function-field form of the Čerednik–Drinfeld comparison: the field $\bar F$ attached to the characteristic-zero Shimura curve is embedded into the function field of the $q$-adically completed integral model, generating it together with the completed coefficient field, linearly disjointly over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, and compatibly with the two Atkin–Lehner maps, with the Galois action of the decomposition group at $q$, and with the two degeneracy maps of the Hecke tower. It is obtained by combining the isomorphism of the completed models with the transfer of the curve model to function fields, and it is used in the assembly of the Mumford-type embedding that feeds the level-lowering argument at $q$.
